3

As in it is "expected" that it would take 3 tries for a non-wizard
with maxed luck to write an unknown spellbook.  For a spellbook that
uses an approximate average of 15 charges per attempt, we have 3 * 15
= 45 charges expected to write the unknown spellbook.

Yes, I understand what expected values are, and I understand that
Jared's post was using expected values.  However, my problem is I
don't think that using expected values is what we should be doing
here.  I think we should be trying to win each game as a separate
event, and I also think that expected values only works if we are
lumping thousands of games together.

Seriously, the point of expected values is to see what happens as the
sample size reaches infinity.  Therefore, if we are trying to see what
happens when the sample size is 1 game, then I really don't think
expected values are useful.


